# Tutorial 4 - synthesis of the serial-parallel multiplier (SPM)
#
#   yosys synth.ys > synth.log
#
# The script is split into the three stages of synthesis, each one leaving
# behind a netlist and a statistics report you can read:
#
#   stage 1  RTL synthesis        SPM.rtl.v          rpt/stat_rtl.txt
#   stage 2  generic gate netlist SPM.unmapped.gl.v  rpt/stat_generic.txt
#   stage 3  technology mapping   SPM.mapped.gl.v    rpt/stat_mapped.txt
#
# Section 6 of the tutorial asks you to compare the three.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0. reading and elaborating the RTL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
read_verilog SPM.v CSADD.v TCMP.v
hierarchy -check

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. RTL synthesis
#
# proc converts the always blocks into logic plus registers. What comes out is
# technology-independent and still word-level: one cell per RTL operator --
# $adff, $and, $or, $xor -- each with a WIDTH parameter, and the module
# hierarchy still intact. This is the "circuit made of generic digital
# building blocks" of section 1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
proc
opt

tee -o rpt/stat_rtl.txt stat

# The Verilog backend runs bmuxmap and demuxmap over the design before writing,
# so it is not read-only. Snapshot the design, write, then restore it, or the
# rest of the flow starts from a different netlist than it would have.
design -save rtl_stage
write_verilog -noattr SPM.rtl.v
design -load rtl_stage

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. logic optimization down to a generic gate-level netlist
#
# synth runs the whole coarse-to-fine script: it re-runs proc, optimizes,
# extracts FSMs and arithmetic, maps memories, then techmaps the word-level
# cells to single-bit ones and calls ABC once with no liberty file. The result
# is generic gates -- $_AND_, $_NAND_, $_OR_, $_XOR_, $_DFF_PP0_ -- with no
# technology attached yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: the module is SPM, not spm. Verilog is case-sensitive and yosys will
# stop with "ERROR: Module `spm' not found!" if the case does not match.
synth -top SPM

# flattening the resultant generic gate level netlist; this removes the hierarchy
flatten; opt

tee -o rpt/stat_generic.txt stat

# Write the synthesized Verilog before mapping it
write_verilog SPM.unmapped.gl.v

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. technology mapping to SKY130
#
# dfflibmap picks a real flip-flop out of the liberty for every generic
# register. abc maps the combinational logic, choosing cell functions and
# drive strengths to meet the delay target it is given:
#
#   -D 1250          target clock period, in picoseconds
#   -constr FILE     boundary conditions: input driving cell, output load
#   -liberty FILE    the cells it may choose from
#
# NOTE the -dont_use flags. The liberty describes 428 cells, and a few of them
# have no business in a plain synthesis flow: the lpflow family are low-power
# cells that need a KAPWR secondary supply or an isolation enable, and
# macro_sparecell is a placeholder for a spare gate. Without these flags ABC
# picks lpflow_inputiso0n_1 as a buffer -- an isolation cell with nothing to
# isolate. See the note after the script in section 5.1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dfflibmap -liberty pdk/lib/sky130_fd_sc_hd__tt_025C_1v80.lib
# -dont_use needs Yosys 0.34 or newer (Ubuntu 24.04 packages 0.33). An older
# Yosys stops with an unknown-option error; drop the two flags and ABC picks an
# lpflow isolation cell as a buffer -- the netlist still simulates, 0.25% larger.
abc -D 1250 -constr abc_constr.txt -liberty pdk/lib/sky130_fd_sc_hd__tt_025C_1v80.lib -dont_use sky130_fd_sc_hd__lpflow_* -dont_use sky130_fd_sc_hd__macro_sparecell

# Clean up and write the synthesized design (gate-level netlist)
opt_clean -purge
write_verilog -noattr -noexpr SPM.mapped.gl.v

# Report design statistics, now with areas taken from the liberty file
tee -o rpt/stat_mapped.txt stat -liberty pdk/lib/sky130_fd_sc_hd__tt_025C_1v80.lib
